# hostapd integrated EAP server — user database for the ROGUE access point (Evil Twin lab).
#
# This file is only read when hostapd.conf has eap_server=1 (the rogue block). The real
# WPA2-Enterprise AP forwards logins to a RADIUS server instead and never looks here.
#
# Phase 1 (outer EAP identity, in the clear): allow any identity to start PEAP.
*               PEAP

# Phase 2 (inner, inside the TLS tunnel): the lab users, with a throwaway password.
#
# The twin does NOT know the real passwords and the login never succeeds. The point is what
# happens on the way to that failure: because these identities are listed, hostapd goes ahead and
# runs the MSCHAPv2 exchange, so the victim station sends its Challenge/Peer-Challenge and, above
# all, its NT-Response — the value derived from the real password. hostapd compares it against the
# throwaway password, the two differ, and it logs "Invalid NT-Response" and fails. But the captured
# Challenge + NT-Response is exactly what an attacker feeds to an offline cracker (asleap, hashcat
# -m 5500) to recover the password at leisure. Run the twin with `start-ap.sh -d` to see it in
# /var/log/wifi/hostapd.log.
#
# A real attack tool (hostapd-wpe) accepts ANY inner identity automatically and needs no such list;
# stock hostapd needs the identity here to proceed to the challenge. The NT-Response it captures is
# the same either way — listing the two lab users just keeps this image stock hostapd.
"sam"           MSCHAPV2        "x"             [2]
"riley"         MSCHAPV2        "x"             [2]
